menu "Pocket VM (diagnostics)"

    config POCKET_VM_SELFTEST
        bool "Enable USB L lifecycle self-test"
        default n
        help
            Runs the host lifecycle test on the device via USB command L.
            Use a private sdkconfig in a dedicated build directory.

    config KSN_DEVICE_PROBE
        bool "Enable USB-triggered Kasane core regression and LCD diagnostic"
        default n
        help
            USB '~' on the home screen runs tests and opaque rectangles. Uses extra
            static storage and stack. Not a production renderer benchmark.

    config POCKET_VM_PROBE
        bool "Enable L0 VM probes (docs/vm/quickjs-freertos-vm-spec.md sec.5)"
        default n
        help
            Off by default and meant to stay that way for every normal build.
            On, this compiles in:
              - main/pocket/vmprobe.c, which samples the guest's JS memory
                usage, free heap, largest free block and ui_task's C stack
                high-water mark every 8th frame (frame time and job counts
                every frame) and logs a rate-limited
                "VMPROBE ..." summary (min/median/max over a ~1s window)
                instead of one line per frame.
              - a #ifdef-guarded probe block inside the vendored
                components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c that exposes
                sizeof(JSStackFrame)/sizeof(JSVarRef) (private to that file)
                and a job-queue depth counter around JS_EnqueueJob /
                JS_ExecutePendingJob.
            The six USB-only L0 workloads that used to live under apps/vmprobe/
            (letters A-F) were retired once L0 finished (tag vm-L0);
            `git checkout vm-L0 -- apps/vmprobe tools/vm_l0_capture.py`
            brings them back for a re-measurement. With this on, the probe
            above now samples whatever app is actually running.
            Build a separate build_* directory with this on -- and give that
            directory its OWN sdkconfig file (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig;
            see sdkconfig.vmprobe.defaults), because ESP-IDF's default
            sdkconfig path is the project root, shared by every build_*
            directory regardless of -B. Never turn this on in
            sdkconfig.defaults or in the shared root sdkconfig.

    config POCKET_VM_RELOC
        bool "Relocatable frame segments: the explicit move API (L3a)"
        default n
        help
            Compiles in JS_VMStackRelocate, which copies the live frame
            segments to fresh addresses and rewrites every pointer that
            named the old ones (docs/vm/vm-L3-design.md, the entry ledger
            is docs/vm/vm-ledger/09-relocation-entries.md).

            Moving is only legal while the VM is parked at an L2c
            safepoint: a running JS_CallInternal keeps pointers into the
            block in C locals and registers, and the spec rules out
            guessing which machine words those are.

            The only caller is the diagnostic one: USB '&' on the home
            screen arms the next app, guest_continue_impl() then moves the
            segments before every resume, and the session logs VM_RELOC
            with what that cost. tools/vmtest/device_reloc.py drives it.
            Unarmed, a RELOC build takes the same path as a build without
            it.

            That is deliberately not a reason to move: deciding WHEN to
            relocate belongs to L4, which has to show first that the
            non-moving segments are not already enough. Off by default and
            meant to stay that way until that case is made.

            Costs, measured on this tree: flash +1,300 B, static DRAM
            +16 B against the same build with it off.

    config POCKET_VM_OOMPROBE
        bool "G12 fragmentation probe: log every failed heap allocation"
        default n
        help
            The device half of the L3/L4 decision (docs/vm/backlog.md G12):
            does fragmentation make allocations fail here? Registers the
            heap's failed-allocation hook, which walks the heap at the moment
            of every refusal and records, per heap region, whether the free
            bytes would have held the request had they been contiguous
            ("G12 FAIL ... cand=1"). Also logs every JS_TakeOOMCanary report
            ("G12 CANARY") and a per-session total ("G12 SESSION").

            Each refusal also says whether treating the VM's frame segments
            as movable would have left a long enough run ("segfix=1"):
            L3/L4 move segments and nothing else, so that is the case they
            could prevent. Adds JS_VMStackBlocks' only caller.

            Adds USB workloads on the home screen that drive the guest into
            OOM on purpose: '!' interleaved holes plus a growing large
            request, '@' a growing array between small objects, '#' JSON
            round-trips of a growing array, and '$' the negative control --
            a heap where the segments are what splits the free space.
            tools/vmtest/device_g12.py runs them and the shipped apps.

            Measurement only. Build a separate build_* directory with its own
            sdkconfig (-D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig).

    config POCKET_UI_TASK_CORE
        int "Core the ui task is pinned to (-1 = let FreeRTOS choose)"
        range -1 1
        default 1
        help
            main/main.c's ui_task owns the whole UI, every screen and the JS
            session (docs/vm/vm-L1-design.md sec.4.1), so where it runs decides
            where every JS job runs. -1 creates it with tskNO_AFFINITY, which
            is what xTaskCreate() did here before; 0 or 1 pins it.

            The default is 1 because that is what the board measured
            (docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8, all 実測(device)): core 1 is
            indistinguishable from no affinity -- the JS turn medians match to
            0.1%, the LCD transfer to 0.04%, DIRAM to the byte -- while core 0
            costs 5.6% of a compute-bound turn, 7.6% of the transfer, and 16%
            of an HTTP request running on the other core, because core 0 is
            where ESP-IDF fixes the Wi-Fi driver task. Pinning also removes the
            one migration the board does take (measured: 1 in 1,800 idle
            frames, docs/vm/vm-L1-report.md sec.8.8).

            The three values are the SAME binary with one immediate changed --
            xTaskCreatePinnedToCore() is the only call site either way -- so a
            measurement across them is not confounded by a code-path change.

endmenu
